Answer an httpd request path that contains a colon with 404 on Windows.
A Windows file name cannot contain a colon: in a path it starts a drive
("C:") or names an alternate data stream ("file::$DATA"). A request for
/C:/data/file.txt was an absolute path, so a client could read any file
on any drive, and /httpd.conf::$DATA or /adm::$INDEX_ALLOCATION/x.htm
reached httpd.conf or a protected directory under a name that the
httpd.conf and password checks did not recognise.

Refuse such paths after they are cleaned, before anything looks at the
file system.

---
 networking/httpd.c |    7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

--- a/networking/httpd.c
+++ b/networking/httpd.c
@@ -2756,6 +2756,13 @@
 		tptr++;
 	}
 
+#if ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
+	/* A colon would make the path absolute (C:/...) or name an
+	 * alternate data stream (httpd.conf::$DATA) */
+	if (strchr(urlcopy, ':'))
+		send_headers_and_exit(HTTP_NOT_FOUND);
+#endif
+
 	/* Log it */
 	if (VERBOSE_2)
 		bb_error_msg("%s %s", prequest, urlcopy);
